WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.530
- [Instructor] What we're
going to do in this video

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:04.280
is talk a little bit about
polynomial identities,

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.630
and this is really just a fancy way

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.730
of seeing whether an expression

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.450
that involves a polynomial is
equal to another expression.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.410
So for example, you're familiar

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:16.930
with x squared plus two x plus one,

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.130
we've seen polynomials
like this multiple times,

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.770
this is a quadratic,
and you might recognize

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:26.060
that this would be equal
to x plus one squared,

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:27.740
that for any value of x,

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:28.810
x squared plus two x plus one

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.700
is the same thing as adding one to that x,

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.430
and then squaring the whole thing.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.580
And we saw this when we first got,

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.290
when we first learned
how to multiply binomials

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.810
and we took the square of binomials,

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.500
but now we're going to do this

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.660
with slightly complicated expressions,

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.890
things that aren't just simple quadratics

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.810
or that might not be as obvious as this.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:51.950
And the way that we're gonna
prove whether they are true

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.090
or not is just with a little
bit of algebraic manipulation.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.480
So for example, if someone
walked up to you on the street

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.250
and said, all right, m
to the third minus one,

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:06.250
is it equal to m minus one
times one plus m plus m squared?

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.610
Pause this video and see what
you would tell that person,

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.750
whether you could prove whether it is

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.773
or is not a true polynomial identity.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:18.670
Okay, let's do it together,

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.150
and the way I would tackle
this is I would expand out,

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.100
I would multiply out what we
have on the right-hand side,

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.040
so this is going to be equal to,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.250
so first, I could take this m

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:28.930
and then multiply it times every term

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.220
in this second expression,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:35.220
so m times one is m, m
times m is m squared,

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:40.950
and then m times m squared
is m to the third power,

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.260
and then I would take this negative one,

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.580
and then multiply and then
distribute that times every term

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:49.860
in that other expression,

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.500
so negative one times one is negative one,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.130
negative one times m is negative m,

00:01:55.130 --> 00:02:00.130
and negative one times m
squared is negative m squared,

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.600
and now let's see if we can simplify this.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.600
We have an m and a negative m,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.330
so those are going to cancel out,

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:10.850
we have an m squared and
a negative m squared,

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.000
so those cancel out, and so we are

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.000
going to be left with m to
the third power, minus one.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.580
Now, clearly, m to the
third power minus one is

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.920
going to be equal to m to the third power

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.410
minus one for any value of m,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.300
these are identical expressions.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.600
So this is, this is indeed
a polynomial identity.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.150
Let's do another example.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.640
Let's say someone were to
walk up to you on the street

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.640
and said, quick, n plus
three squared plus two n,

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.410
is that equal to eight n plus 13,

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.550
is this a polynomial identity?

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:49.590
Pause this video and see
if you can figure that out.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:52.600
All right, now we're gonna
work on that together.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.040
And I would do it the exact same way.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.180
I would try to simplify with
a little bit of algebra,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.750
maybe the easiest thing to do first,

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.950
and you could do this in multiple ways is,

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.430
I have this, I have these n terms,

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:05.480
two n's here, eight n's over here,

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.490
well, what if I were to
get these two n's out

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.950
of the left-hand side, so if I were

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:13.100
to just subtract two n from
both sides of this equation,

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.630
I'm going to get on the left-hand side,

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:19.800
n plus three squared, and
on the right-hand side,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.800
I'm going to get six n,
eight n minus two n, plus 13.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.230
Now, what's n plus three squared?

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.890
Well, that's going to be n squared

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:32.160
plus two times three times n.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.430
And if what I just did does
not seem familiar to you,

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:37.050
I encourage you to look

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.670
at the videos about squaring binomials,

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.870
but this is going to be plus six n,

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.900
plus three squared, which is nine,

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.270
and is this going to be
equal to six n plus 13?

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.440
Well, already this is
starting to look a little bit,

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.370
a little bit sketchy, but let's

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.720
just keep going with the algebra.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.570
So let's see if we subtract six n

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.783
from both sides, what do you get?

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:02.810
Well, on the left-hand side,

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.340
you're just going to
have n squared plus nine,

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.810
and on the right-hand side,
you're going to get 13.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.160
Now, are there values of n for

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.520
which this is not always true?

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:16.353
Well, sure.

00:04:16.353 --> 00:04:18.230
I can find a lot of values of n for

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:19.720
which this is not always true.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.760
If n is a zero, this is
not going to be true.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.270
If n is one, this is not going to be true.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:27.060
If n is two, this actually would be true,

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.810
but if n is three, this
is not going to be true.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.310
If n is four, five, et cetera,

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:32.610
so for actually most values of n,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.090
this is not going to be true.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.420
So in order for it to be
a polynomial identity,

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.680
it has to be true for all of the values

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.350
that are legitimate values that
you can evaluate for those,

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.410
for the variable in question.

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:48.820
So this one right over
here is not a polynomial,

00:04:50.379 --> 00:04:55.379
polynomial identity, and we are done.

